<sbb-container background-expanded color="milk" class="lp-container">
  <div class="sbb-grid-only">
    <div class="inset">
      <sbb-title level="2" visual-level="2">Migrating to Lyne.</sbb-title>
      <sbb-accordion>
        <sbb-expansion-panel color="white">
          <sbb-expansion-panel-header>Do I have to migrate my product?</sbb-expansion-panel-header>
          <sbb-expansion-panel-content>That depends on the lifecycle of your product or platform. If your product will be in operation beyond 2028, we recommend migrating – primarily because the Angular library will no longer be maintained after that. If your product reaches its end of life before 2029, a migration is hardly worthwhile.</sbb-expansion-panel-content>
        </sbb-expansion-panel>
        <sbb-expansion-panel color="white">
          <sbb-expansion-panel-header>When is a good time to migrate?</sbb-expansion-panel-header>
          <sbb-expansion-panel-content>There is no general recommendation for the ideal time to migrate. If larger conceptual changes are planned anyway – such as reworking several views – it can be worth carrying out the migration before or at the same time. Either way, the migration is a chance to consider improvements to usability and user experience right away. The <sbb-link href="mailto:ux@sbb.ch">UX Service</sbb-link> will be happy to advise you.</sbb-expansion-panel-content>
        </sbb-expansion-panel>
        <sbb-expansion-panel color="white">
          <sbb-expansion-panel-header>Is there technical support?</sbb-expansion-panel-header>
          <sbb-expansion-panel-content>We expect to clarify the needs for migration support in more detail in Q4 2026. If there is broad demand, we will build tools in Q1 2027 that help with a migration or automate certain tasks.</sbb-expansion-panel-content>
        </sbb-expansion-panel>
        <sbb-expansion-panel color="white">
          <sbb-expansion-panel-header>How long will the old Angular library be supported?</sbb-expansion-panel-header>
          <sbb-expansion-panel-content>The old <sbb-link href="https://angular.app.sbb.ch" target="_blank">Angular library</sbb-link> will be maintained until 31 December 2028. After that, the library remains available, but maintenance ends. If your product or platform has a longer lifecycle, a migration is recommended.</sbb-expansion-panel-content>
        </sbb-expansion-panel>
        <sbb-expansion-panel color="white">
          <sbb-expansion-panel-header>Can I use the old Angular library and Lyne at the same time?</sbb-expansion-panel-header>
          <sbb-expansion-panel-content>No. The libraries are not compatible and interfere with each other.</sbb-expansion-panel-content>
        </sbb-expansion-panel>
      </sbb-accordion>
      <p>Not sure when or whether to migrate? The <sbb-link href="mailto:ux@sbb.ch">UX Service</sbb-link> will be happy to advise you.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</sbb-container>

<sbb-container background-expanded color="white" class="lp-container">
  <div class="sbb-grid-only">
    <div class="inset">
      <sbb-title level="2" visual-level="2">5 reasons for Lyne.</sbb-title>
      <ol class="sbb-step-list sbb-text-m">
        <li>
          <strong>Consistency.</strong>
          <p class="subtext">Interactions, look and feel, design and patterns are the same everywhere. This reduces the learning effort for customers and employees.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Efficient collaboration.</strong>
          <p class="subtext">Shared standards speed up development and make it easier for UX, POs, business and development to work together. Recurring design and implementation questions have already been solved, and teams are better aligned from the start.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accessibility.</strong>
          <p class="subtext">All components meet the current accessibility standards according to WCAG 2.2.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Flexible components.</strong>
          <p class="subtext">Components are available in several sizes and can be adapted to different usage scenarios. This also allows complex user interfaces for expert applications.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technology independence.</strong>
          <p class="subtext">Lyne can be used with Angular, React and other front-end stacks – or without any framework at all.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
</sbb-container>

